\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Pauli arrays of the flip and the sign}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{At #{\ui\varepsilon_1}, where #{\varepsilon_j} is the standard basis
of #{\mathbb C^n}, the spatial form has value #{-1}, while the
Hermitian norm squared is #{1}. Choose #{S=(\mathbb C^2)^{\otimes n}}
as in the [centered Dirac operator](fdip-000R), with the ordered
basis #{(e_0,e_1)} of each factor. The flip of that factor exchanges
the two basis vectors, and the sign of that factor fixes #{e_0} and
sends #{e_1} to #{-e_1}. For #{1\le j\le n}, the operator #{\gamma_j}
applies the sign to each factor before #{j}, the flip to factor
#{j}, and the identity to each later factor. An empty collection of
factors contributes the scalar identity. For #{n=1}, #{\gamma_1} is
the flip of the single factor.}

\p{In the [Pauli convention](fcap-002L), the flip and the sign have
arrays
##{X=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
Z=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix}.}
With #{\bbI=\bbI_2}, the product-index array of #{\gamma_j} is
##{\gamma_j=Z^{\otimes(j-1)}\otimes X
\otimes \bbI^{\otimes(n-j)}.}
An empty tensor product contributes the scalar identity. This array
is the matrix of the represented generator
#{\rho(\iota(\varepsilon_j))} in \ref{fdip-000R}. The
identities #{X^{\dagger}=X}, #{Z^{\dagger}=Z}, #{X^2=Z^2=\bbI}, #{XZ=-ZX} and the
Clifford relation are proved in \ref{fdip-00L8}. Mathlib's
\leanref{Matrix.kronecker},
\leanref{Matrix.mul_kronecker_mul},
\leanref{Matrix.conjTranspose_kronecker} and
\leanref{Matrix.one_kronecker_one} give the corresponding matrix
construction. Iterating them requires identifying the product-index
bases.}
